The table structure information is ({database name: {schema name: [table name]}}): 
{'bigquery-public-data': {'san_francisco': ['film_locations', 'bikeshare_stations', 'street_trees', 'sfpd_incidents', 'bikeshare_status', 'bikeshare_trips', '311_service_requests', 'sffd_service_calls']}}
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their possible roles

bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips  
• bike_number ­– Grouping key. We want a total distance for every individual bike.  
• start_station_id / end_station_id – Join keys to pull lat / lon from the stations table.  
• duration_sec – (Optional) sanity check; could throw‑away trips of 0 seconds, etc.  
• start_date / end_date – (Optional) time filters if you only want certain years.

bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations  
• station_id ­– Join key back to trips.  
• latitude / longitude ­– Coordinates used to compute point‑to‑point (“as‑the‑crow‑flies”) distance for each leg of a trip.  
• landmark / name – (Optional) for readability in diagnostics.

Typical conditions that may be applied

• start_station_id IS NOT NULL AND end_station_id IS NOT NULL  
• s1.latitude  IS NOT NULL AND s2.latitude IS NOT NULL  
• duration_sec > 0                                 -- optional scrub  
• bike_number IS NOT NULL                           -- defensive  
• You can also ignore trips whose start & end stations are identical (distance = 0).

Ten exploratory → increasingly complete BigQuery SELECTs
(Each one limited to 20 rows for sampling / debugging purposes.)

```sql
-- Description: Peek at some bike numbers that exist in the trips table.
SELECT DISTINCT bike_number
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`
WHERE bike_number IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- Description: Inspect a handful of stations with their co‑ordinates.
SELECT station_id, name, latitude, longitude
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- Description: Join one trip’s start station to pull its latitude / longitude.
SELECT t.trip_id,
       t.bike_number,
       t.start_station_id,
       s.latitude  AS start_lat,
       s.longitude AS start_lon
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips` AS t
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` AS s
  ON   t.start_station_id = s.station_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- Description: Same join but for the end_station_id →
SELECT t.trip_id,
       t.bike_number,
       t.end_station_id,
       s.latitude  AS end_lat,
       s.longitude AS end_lon
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips` AS t
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` AS s
  ON   t.end_station_id = s.station_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- Description: Bring BOTH start & end co‑ordinates into the same row.
SELECT t.trip_id,
       t.bike_number,
       s1.latitude  AS start_lat,
       s1.longitude AS start_lon,
       s2.latitude  AS end_lat,
       s2.longitude AS end_lon
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips` AS t
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` AS s1
  ON   t.start_station_id = s1.station_id
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` AS s2
  ON   t.end_station_id = s2.station_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- Description: Compute raw great‑circle distance (meters) for each sampled trip.
SELECT t.trip_id,
       t.bike_number,
       ROUND(
         ST_DISTANCE(
           ST_GEOGPOINT(s1.longitude, s1.latitude),
           ST_GEOGPOINT(s2.longitude, s2.latitude)
         )
       , 1) AS meters
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`     AS t
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`  AS s1
  ON   t.start_station_id = s1.station_id
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`  AS s2
  ON   t.end_station_id   = s2.station_id
WHERE  s1.latitude  IS NOT NULL 
  AND  s2.latitude  IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- Description: Same as above but convert meters → miles (1 mile ≈ 1609.34 m).
SELECT t.trip_id,
       t.bike_number,
       ROUND(
         ST_DISTANCE(
           ST_GEOGPOINT(s1.longitude, s1.latitude),
           ST_GEOGPOINT(s2.longitude, s2.latitude)
         ) / 1609.34
       , 3) AS miles
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`     AS t
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`  AS s1
  ON   t.start_station_id = s1.station_id
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`  AS s2
  ON   t.end_station_id   = s2.station_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- Description: Aggregate total distance per bike (sample of 20 bikes).
SELECT bike_number,
       ROUND(SUM(
         ST_DISTANCE(
           ST_GEOGPOINT(s1.longitude, s1.latitude),
           ST_GEOGPOINT(s2.longitude, s2.latitude)
         ) / 1609.34
       ), 2) AS total_miles
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`     AS t
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`  AS s1
  ON   t.start_station_id = s1.station_id
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`  AS s2
  ON   t.end_station_id   = s2.station_id
WHERE  s1.latitude IS NOT NULL AND s2.latitude IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY bike_number
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- Description: Add a trip‑count to each bike to inspect correlation.
SELECT bike_number,
       COUNT(*)                                      AS trips,
       ROUND(SUM(
         ST_DISTANCE(
           ST_GEOGPOINT(s1.longitude, s1.latitude),
           ST_GEOGPOINT(s2.longitude, s2.latitude)
         ) / 1609.34
       ), 2)                                         AS total_miles
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`     AS t
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`  AS s1
  ON   t.start_station_id = s1.station_id
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`  AS s2
  ON   t.end_station_id   = s2.station_id
WHERE  s1.latitude IS NOT NULL AND s2.latitude IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY bike_number
ORDER  BY total_miles DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- Description: FINAL query – total distance (miles) for every bike in the system.
